the thing about the space station is  it's just so dynamic so much is  happening on a daily basis i get the  emails every morning that tell me what  the crew are doing what the changes the  space the space station are what's gone  wrong with the space station overnight  every night you know every day things  are going wrong with troubleshooting  things it's been up there for 15 years  now and anyone who's had any complex  machinery or a house or a car or  anything for 15 years you know that you  need to keep on top of it and keep  things moving but also the science has  changed not just with every six month  mission but all the time every time you  see a visiting vehicle going up to the  space station that will have some  science on it and we're now having  spacex return with with scientific  samples from the space station as well  so it's such a dynamic environment it's  not a case of all the space stations up  there i wonder what it's doing now it's  doing different things every day and the  science is continually changing do you  know much about the science you have to  do yet does that come later or are you  already training for the experiments in  the science you do as well there's a  couple of different elements here  firstly we get trained on all the  payloads on board the space station so  in the columbus laboratory the u.s lab  the  japanese laboratory for example there  are fixed payloads we know how to  operate them so we can be effective  operators of those payloads and yet the  science can change  and really we're installing science  we're running the experiments and we're  returning the samples we can do all of  that without actually having to know  anything about the science itself  obviously  you know we like to be interested in the  science it makes it much more fun if you  understand what you're doing and you  really understand the benefits of what  you're doing so we try and learn as much  about the science as possible but we are  by no means the experts on the science  itself  also then there's the human physiology  science experiments there's there are  about 30 experiments that i've signed up  for that i'll be doing on my own body  that'll be taking a lot of blood a lot  of urine for example saliva  looking at my gut my immune system  my intercranial pressure for example  we're studying all these things uh  ultrasounds of the eye to see um how the  pressure inside your head can perhaps  affect your eyesight during six months  in zero gravity uh all of this i find  absolutely fascinating again and you  know i think every astronaut wants to  know what's going to happen to your body  during that period how do you feel about  being that that test dummy though like  that's quite invasive it's hot you know  i know you've signed on for it but does  it do you feel anything about it or it's  just no i think you know we we really  get to know the scientists very well who  are running those experiments um and  they install a lot of confidence in us  that what we're doing is not only safe  but it's also very beneficial and like  you said it's a sacrifice you make you  know you're in a very unique and very  privileged position  and so to to go up for six months the  space station it's a small sacrifice to  make to kind of hand over your body for  that period to try and get as much  valuable medical information as we can  you can fail you can do things wrong but  can you excel i mean as an astronaut can  you just do the right job with the  minimum mistakes or is there room to to  excel and like think wow he did even  better than we imagined or is it just a  case of minimum mistakes for an  astronaut uh i think it's probably the  latter  i'm sure you've heard of the astronaut  prayer of you know god just please don't  let me mess up it's one of those cases  that there are there are many  opportunities where you can do things  wrong  and that's what the training is for  that's why we train for two and a half  years before our mission is so that we  don't hopefully do anything wrong and  the crew tend to train to quite an equal  level on on all tasks so everybody can  do a spacewalk everyone can operate the  robotic arm for example to give you  maximum flexibility but you know you're  right  the whole program is fraught with uh  human error possibilities so our job is  to make sure that we always remain on  the ball and we and we try not to make  any mistakes or minimize the mistakes  i see the positive about that from  really a pr thing you open up space  flight to  an audience that perhaps wouldn't  otherwise have looked at space as an  interesting thing and so they bring with  them a large element of outreach and of  pr and they can and they can also from a  personal perspective they come from a  very different viewpoint from many of  the space tourist artists and an artist  will view space and see things very  differently to a scientist an engineer  or a pilot and that perspective is  unique and it's very valuable to have um  and of course you know the tourists only  fly when there's absolutely no impact to  the science program there's no impact to  the operations on board the space  station so there are no negative effects  to flying a space tourist tim peake will  start a long duration mission blasting  off from baikonur 2015.  like his colleagues he's taking language  and theory classes at star city and has  learned how to put on the sokul  spacesuit in the cramped environment of  the soyuz orbital stage i've heard that  the trainees is incredibly accurate so  much so i mean we spend so much time in  the modules you see behind me  familiarizing ourselves with what the  inside of the space station looks like  and i was speaking to samantha  christopheretti recently who you know  who's been up on board the space station  and heard that  actually it feels very bizarre because  suddenly you're you're in an environment  you've spent many many hours in um but  you're actually in space this time and  not in building nine where i'm stood at  the moment um so the training is very  good at simulating what it's going to be  like i guess on training we're probably  training about 40 45 hours per week as  you get into the final six months of the  flow obviously you need to pace yourself  you you can't get on board the space  station exhausted you need to arrive on  board the space station ready for six  months of really hard work so you do  need to pace yourself but i think the  training program does a very good job of  that as a uk astronaut who's being  sponsored by the uk government now the  uk government's joined the human  spaceflight program i'm really delighted  about that so i want this to continue i  want the uk to continue its  participation in human space flight and  hopefully that will bring benefit for  british scientists and for british  industry and engineers but more  importantly i hope it will have a really  positive impact on kids and the younger  generation to try and inspire  the younger generation the science and  technology and engineering is not only  exciting but there are great career  opportunities in those fields as well no  one nation can probably go to mars it's  going to take a combined effort so i  think it's really important that we  understand that and as we all take  ownership of that and so to try and you  know bring the uk along and to continue  its participation in in the future  missions that are coming up is very  important do you get a ribbing from the  american astronauts for being british do  they make jokes at your expense or are  they all they or is it great because  they love the british accent what do  they like with that no they're all great  to work with very positive and no i  probably get more of a ribbing from my  european colleagues than the americans  um i think that that's a great question  i've never been asked that before but i  think the timing is is perfect because  um you obviously need a certain amount  of time to feel confident and to feel  prepared extraordinary they didn't land  they just orbited around the moon  imagine what it must have been like to  see that with your naked eyes that's  terrifying yeah see terrifying

